Subject: Recover template arguments libclang gives no cursor for

`instantiate_template` reconstructs a specialization's arguments by walking the
cursors under the use site, because libclang exposes template arguments
properly only for function templates. Not every argument has a cursor there. A
builtin has none, so `h2<T, int>` puts one `TypeRef` where two arguments were
written, and neither does an argument left to its default, so `holder<T>`
against `template <class U, class D = U> struct holder` finds one where the
declaration has two. Either way the count comes up short of
`num_self_template_params`, `instantiate_template` gives up, and
`builtin_or_resolved_ty` falls back on the bare template declaration.

That fallback does not produce a usable binding. The enclosing template loses
the parameter it passed in - nothing uses it any more - and the field is written
as the template declaration's own name applied to the declaration's own
parameters, which are bound nowhere in the emitted struct:

    template <class U, class D = U> struct holder { D* p; };
    template <class T> struct outer { holder<T> value; };

becomes `pub struct outer { pub value: holder<D> }`, which does not compile.

libclang will name the arguments itself through
`clang_Type_getTemplateArgumentAsType`, defaults included once the canonical
type is chained on - that is what `TemplateInstantiation::from_ty` already asks,
and it is now shared as `template_arg_types`. Ask it when, and only when, the
cursor walk came up with the wrong number: the walk stays the primary source,
so nothing which parses today changes.

What libclang hands back is each argument's *type*, not the cursor it was
written at, and an argument which is a parameter of the enclosing template
arrives as an unexposed type with no declaration - too little to build a
`TypeParam` from, and it would become an opaque blob, losing the enclosing
template's parameter as surely as the bare declaration did. Those arguments are
the ones the cursor walk did find, so they are taken from it, under both the
name they were written under and clang's canonical spelling for them, since a
defaulted argument arrives as `type-parameter-0-0` rather than as `T`.

Two things this does not recover, neither of which the bare declaration
recovered either. An argument which is a parameter under a qualifier or an
operator - `holder<const T>`, `holder<T*>` - is spelled as neither of those two
keys, so the parameter under it goes opaque: the argument's own shape survives,
a pointer argument stays a pointer, but the enclosing template loses the
parameter, while the instantiation and its other arguments come through. And a
non-type argument comes back from the API as an invalid type, filtered out here
as it already is in `from_ty`, so the count stays short and the instantiation is
still abandoned - which is the existing treatment of const-value arguments a few
lines above.

Addresses the bug reported as rust-lang/rust-bindgen#3161.

